VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: RC3
ATTEMPT: 1
---
Setup: cp -a of /home/darrell/bin/ai/budget2/.worktrees/rollover-schedule at commit
1f9a35a (parent ee7af8a=RC2, base bcb6226=master). Oracle (.swarm/tier3/RC3/accept.sh)
re-run in the copy: ORACLE PASS, `go build`/`go vet` clean, shipped suites green,
testdata untouched. Manifest matches `git show --stat 1f9a35a`.

REFUTED — criterion 4 (round trip / untouched re-save) and criterion 3 (truthful
display), reproducible defect:

A plan with UseCurrentMonth=true that has rolled far enough forward that an
expense's (or income's) StartMonth AND EndMonth both clamp to 0 — the documented
"already finished, contributing nothing" state that `shiftScheduleOffsets`
(internal/services/retirement/settings_current_month.go:49) deliberately produces
and explicitly does NOT remove — renders and behaves incorrectly:

1. Row text is self-contradictory: "Starts Sep 2026 · Through Aug 2026 (the last
   month charged)" — Through is BEFORE Starts, for an item the code comments say is
   simply over. (throughMonth(EndMonth=0) = -1, one month before plan start.)
2. The inline edit form's own rendered `end_month` value ("2026-08") is earlier
   than its own `min` ("2026-09"), i.e. the form renders an out-of-range value.
   htmx submits it anyway on ANY field change in the form (hx-trigger="change
   delay:500ms, input delay:500ms from:find input[type=number]" — so toggling
   the inflation checkbox or editing the amount submits the whole form, not just
   the touched field).
3. Because handleWhatIfUpdateExpense unconditionally calls parseMonthRange on
   EVERY update regardless of which field changed, that untouched end_month
   value is re-validated against "before plan start" and REJECTED with a 400:
   "Through month must be Sep 2026 or later (the plan starts that month)".
   The user cannot toggle inflation/discretionary or edit the amount on this
   row at all without first fixing (moving) a date they never touched.

Reproduced with a real httptest handler call against the actual code (not a
prototype-only claim): built a plan with StartDate 20 months in the past,
ExpenseSource StartMonth=2/EndMonth=5 (fully elapsed), loaded it (triggering the
real rollover shift to StartMonth=0/EndMonth=0), rendered /whatif, extracted the
row's own rendered start_month/end_month input values via string search, and
POSTed them back unchanged (+ inflation=on) to handleWhatIfUpdateExpense:

  after rollover: StartMonth=0 EndMonth=0 StartDate=2026-09
  end input value="2026-08" min="2026-09"
  startVal="2026-09" endVal="2026-08"
  re-save status=400 body: "Through month must be Sep 2026 or later
    (the plan starts that month)"
  row text: "Starts Sep 2026 · Through Aug 2026 (the last month charged)"

This is squarely in RC3's scope: RC3 (this commit) is what wires
`parseMonthRange`'s before-plan-start rejection into every income/expense save
(criterion 2) and is what introduced the exact-round-trip guarantee for the
inline edit forms (criterion 4: "an untouched edit-form re-save never moves a
date"). The clamp-to-0 rollover state itself predates RC3 (RC2), but RC2 never
exposed it through a re-validated month form — RC3 is the commit that turns a
pre-existing quiet data state into a save-blocking, self-contradictory UI. The
attack-angle brief for this checker explicitly named this exact case ("An
expense whose EndMonth clamped to 0 ... can the inline edit form even be
re-submitted?") — it is not a stretch reading of scope.

The oracle's O2b round-trip test only exercises a still-future entry
(StartMonth 11, EndMonth 24, both positive after the plan's `rc3Fixture`); it
never constructs a StartMonth=0/EndMonth=0 post-clamp fixture, so it does not
catch this. Income sources share the identical `parseMonthRange` /
`throughMonth` / `shiftRange` code paths as expenses, so the same defect
applies there too (not separately re-proven, but the code is structurally
identical — same helper functions, same template pattern in
income-sources-list.html).

Other criteria checked and NOT refuted:
- Criterion 1 (month inputs, min attribute, labels): present on all four forms;
  add-form min/value both derive from CalendarMonth; confirmed by direct read of
  income-sources-list.html and month_input.go.
- Criterion 2 (parsing/validation, one helper): parseMonthOffset/parseMonthRange
  is the single conversion path via retirement.MonthOffset -> monthsBetween;
  before-start, inverted-range, blank, unparseable all correctly produce 400 in
  the *reachable, non-clamped* cases the oracle tests.
- Criterion 5 (rollover + display agreement): oracle's
  TestRC3OracleRolloverDisplay passed in my copy; re-verified the underlying
  months-between/shift arithmetic.
- Criterion 6 (tests, `go test -count=1 ./...`, `make check`): ran the shipped
  suites named in the oracle; all green in my copy.
- Dual-formatter sweep: grepped every "2006-01"/"Jan 2006" producer/consumer in
  Go, templates and JS. Found `internal/services/retirement/analysis/spending_funding.go`
  (local `calendarMonthLabel` returning "2006-01", NOT the human label) feeding
  `web/static/js/whatif-spending-optimizer.js` ("Calendar month" table column
  showing raw ISO strings) — a real second formatter of the same StartMonth
  figure elsewhere on the What-If page. Confirmed via `git log` that this file
  and its JS consumer were last touched in the SP2/spending-first-optimizer
  commits, NOT in RC2 or RC3 (`git show --stat 1f9a35a` does not include them,
  and they are absent from the RC3.1.files manifest). Per CLAUDE.md scope rule
  this is OUT OF SCOPE for RC3 (pre-existing, untouched code) — reported below
  as an observation, not grounds for FAIL.
- Timeline chart events (handlers.go ~683-735, "Pension starts" at a fractional
  year, X-axis in `buildProjectionChartEvents`): this positions chart markers on
  a numeric year axis and carries no calendar-month TEXT (label is "Pension
  starts", no date); it is not one of the four schedule-list/form surfaces
  criterion 3 names, and the file's fractional-year arithmetic there predates
  RC3 (see ruling 2026-09-16a, F-078 note). Not a criterion violation.

Observations (not FAIL, backlog candidates):
- `internal/services/retirement/analysis/spending_funding.go`'s local
  `calendarMonthLabel(month time.Time) string` returns ISO "2006-01", not the
  "Jan 2006" human label, and is rendered directly as a table cell ("Calendar
  month" column, "Observed configured events" table) in
  web/static/js/whatif-spending-optimizer.js:400,405. Same figure (an income
  source's StartMonth) as the schedule rows, rendered in two different formats
  on the same What-If page depending on which sub-view you're in. Pre-existing,
  out of RC3's manifest — flag for a future task, not this one.
- The clamp-to-0 "already finished, contributing nothing" state (RC2 design)
  combined with RC3's strict re-validation is the root cause above; a fix would
  need either (a) parseMonthRange to tolerate a Through value at/after
  max(planStart, StartMonth-anchored actual date) when the value is unchanged
  from the rendered one, or (b) the clamp to stop producing an inverted range
  (e.g. clamp EndMonth to at least StartMonth+1, or omit the Through
  input/text and show "ended" once EndMonth<=StartMonth), or (c) auto-remove
  fully-elapsed entries on rollover. That design decision belongs to the lead,
  not this checker.
